### 2.4 Android readiness assessment (verified at this commit)

- Already portable: `taskbar.rs` has `#[cfg(not(target_os = "windows"))]` no-op stubs; the `windows` crate is target-gated in `Cargo.toml`; path/URI helpers already carry non-Windows branches; the mobile entry-point attribute is in place; the pure-Rust crates (`ureq`, `sha2`, `minisign-verify`, `base64`, `encoding_rs`, `percent-encoding`, `serde`, `getrandom`) contain no platform-specific code on their face and are expected to compile for Android targets — a review finding, not a build result; R4's CI job must actually compile them before anyone claims Android support.
- Blocks compilation on Android: `rfd` 0.15 (its official platform list is macOS, Windows, Linux, WASM32; no Android backend) and `tauri-plugin-single-instance` (desktop-only).
- Desktop-only by design: all 11 windowing commands, the taskbar thumbnail controls, the Miracast launcher, argv/file-association launch, OS drag-and-drop, window geometry persistence, NSIS bundling.
- Behavior changes from WebView2 to Android WebView: `navigator.mediaSession` is absent in Android WebView (Chromium disables the Media Session API in WebView; MDN browser-compat data lists it unsupported), `HTMLMediaElement.setSinkId` is not available, `showDirectoryPicker` is not available, and codec support follows the device's platform decoders instead of bundled Chromium codecs.

## 3. Architecture record (Phase 1 documentation deliverable)

As the first work item of Phase 1, write the architecture record into the repository (for example `docs/android-architecture.md`): the D1-D10 decisions with their one-line rationale copied from Part 1; the phase boundaries from Parts 4-7; the `MediaRef` file model (R10); the V1 protocol and V2 player-surface verification results once they exist; the final permission list per phase; and any fallback activated during the work (for example the D2 player-surface fallback). Keep it updated as phases complete. It is a record of decisions already made, not a proposal awaiting approval.

## 4. Phase 1 (owner's Phase 1: core playback, browsing, gestures, resume)

Build a stable video/audio player with folder and file browsing, on-screen touch gestures (volume, brightness, seek, screen lock, aspect ratio, rotation), and resume from last position.

- **R1.** Install the Android toolchain per the official Tauri v2 prerequisites: Android Studio; `JAVA_HOME` pointing at the bundled JBR; SDK Platform, Platform-Tools, NDK (side by side), Build-Tools, and Command-line Tools via SDK Manager; `ANDROID_HOME` and `NDK_HOME` environment variables; Rust targets `aarch64-linux-android`, `armv7-linux-androideabi`, `i686-linux-android`, `x86_64-linux-android` via rustup.
- **R2.** Run `npm run tauri android init` from `desktop/` to generate `src-tauri/gen/android`. Commit the generated project but gitignore `keystore.properties` and `local.properties`.
- **R3.** **Verify:** `tauri android dev` runs on an API 33 emulator and on one physical device, with WebView inspection through `chrome://inspect`.
- **R4.** CI: add an Android job to `.github/workflows/ci.yml` that builds a debug APK (`tauri android build --apk`) on `ubuntu-latest`; leave the existing Windows jobs untouched.
- **R5.** Move `rfd` 0.15 behind `[target.'cfg(not(target_os = "android"))'.dependencies]` and gate `tauri-plugin-single-instance` with `cfg(not(target_os = "android"))`. Android is not single-task by design: apps can hold multiple tasks in Recents, and activity instances multiply unless the launch mode restricts them. Single-instance semantics on Android come from the manifest (`android:launchMode="singleTask"` on the main activity) plus `onNewIntent` routing new launches to the running task, which is why the desktop plugin has no Android role. Replace rfd's three call sites (`aaz_select_folder`, `aaz_select_files`, `aaz_save_snapshot`) with the Android implementations in R11, R12, and R16.
- **R6.** Gate the desktop-only commands (11 windowing commands, `aaz_taskbar_update_state`, `aaz_cast_open_windows_cast`) behind cfg so they compile to stubs returning a "not supported on this platform" error on Android, mirroring the existing `taskbar.rs` no-op pattern. Extend `desktop-bridge.js` with a platform flag so the frontend feature-detects instead of assuming presence.
- **R7.** Extract the `aazmedia://` handler's HTTP/Range core (strict Range parsing, capped reads, allowlist enforcement) into a platform-neutral module shared by the desktop protocol, the Android protocol registration, and the R44 LAN cast server. The core is std-only and expected to compile unchanged for Android targets, which R4 must prove with an actual build — expected is not verified. Register the custom protocol on Android exactly as on desktop for web-loaded resources (subtitle files, artwork, any web-embedded media), then run V1.
- **V1 (Verify).** On an API 33 emulator, load a 100 MB or larger MP4 through `aazmedia://` and issue 20 randomized Range requests (start, middle, tail, open-ended), diffing returned bytes against the source file. Playback itself does not depend on this protocol (Media3 reads fds directly, R13); the protocol serves web-loaded resources. If the custom protocol fails on Android WebView, fall back to serving those resources from a 127.0.0.1 HTTP server that reuses the existing range-streaming implementation, and record which path shipped in the architecture record.
- **V2 (Verify).** On API 26 and API 34+ devices or emulators: (a) the Media3 FFmpeg decoder extension, manually built with only the `ac3`, `eac3`, `dca`, and `mlp`/`truehd` decoders enabled and passing the R14 license/codec compliance check, loads and decodes AC-3 and DTS samples; (b) the native video surface layers correctly with the Tauri WebView (z-order, resize, rotation, fullscreen transitions); (c) a content URI from the R11 picker plays through the R13 fd pipeline with working seek. If the extension build fails, those formats drop to the R15 unsupported-track notice; if surface layering fails, activate the D2 surface fallback. Record results in the architecture record.
- **R8.** `tauri.conf.json`: add the `bundle.android` configuration; keep identifier `com.aaz.player`; set `minSdkVersion` 24 per D4 with runtime gates.
- **R9.** `aaz_list_system_fonts` shells out to PowerShell/registry on Windows (already cfg-gated). On Android, return the bundled font list plus "System default". Do not shell out on Android.
- **R10.** Introduce a `MediaRef` abstraction used by both Rust and JS: `{ kind: "path" | "contentUri" | "mediaStoreUri", value, displayName, size?, mtimeMs?, folderKey? }`. Desktop keeps `kind: "path"` unchanged. Update the canonical identity keys in `js/modules/track-identity.js` to use `MediaRef` so identical basenames in different folders stay distinct (preserving the existing fix).
- **R11.** File picking: implement multi-file picking in the Kotlin SAF plugin with `ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT` (not `ACTION_GET_CONTENT`: only document intents offer persistable grants), adding `CATEGORY_OPENABLE`, `FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION`, and `FLAG_GRANT_PERSISTABLE_URI_PERMISSION` to the picker intent. After each pick, call `takePersistableUriPermission(uri, takeFlags)` with `takeFlags` masked from the returned intent's own grant flags, wrapped in try/catch `SecurityException` — providers are not required to offer persistable grants, so record per item whether access is persisted or session-only (an untaken grant lasts only until reboot). Android caps persisted grants per app: release grants with `releasePersistableUriPermission` when items leave the library. A moved or deleted document invalidates its grant even after taking; route that to the designed re-grant error state. `tauri-plugin-dialog` is supported on Android and may serve as a fallback picker, but treat its grants as session-only; the Kotlin picker is the shipping path for the library.
- **R12.** Folder browsing: `tauri-plugin-dialog` does not support folder picking on Android (per the official plugin docs), so implement a Kotlin plugin command around `ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT_TREE`, persisting the returned tree grant under the same rules and failure handling as R11, and traverse the tree with `DocumentsContract` to enumerate the existing extension sets (`AUDIO_EXTS`, `VIDEO_EXTS`, `SUBTITLE_EXTS`, `PLAYLIST_EXTS`). This powers folder browsing in Phase 1 and the full library in Phase 2.
- **R13.** Media serving from content URIs, with explicit fd ownership. Media3 reads picked media through `ContentDataSource` for `content://` URIs with random access, or a `FileDescriptorDataSource` over a detached fd. The Kotlin fd layer owns the lifecycle: `ContentResolver.openFileDescriptor(uri, "r")` returns a `ParcelFileDescriptor` owned by the plugin; the plugin detaches the fd and hands it to Rust or Media3 exactly once; the receiver closes it exactly once (in Rust, `File::from_raw_fd` takes ownership — no leaks, no double-close); the empty ParcelFileDescriptor shell is closed after detach. Read the fd directly: never reopen it through `/proc/self/fd/<fd>`, which platform policy does not guarantee openable for provider-owned files. Detect non-seekable providers (pipe or socket fds: check `fstat` file type and whether `lseek` fails) and unknown sizes (`st_size` of 0 or -1): serve Range requests only over seekable regular files; for non-seekable sources either copy to a bounded app-cache file first or play as a non-seekable stream with seeking disabled in the UI, recording per-provider behavior in the probe matrix. The cast LAN server and, while V1 stands, the `aazmedia://` protocol read through the same fd source layer, preserving the existing protections: authorize tree/document URIs at pick time and revalidate per request, enforce extension and MIME checks, keep strict Range parsing and the existing size caps. The Phase 1 slice of the R35 player bridge lands here as a prerequisite: a Kotlin plugin skeleton exposing load/play/pause/seekTo/getState plus `aaz:player-state` events over a Media3 player — enough for V2 and the Phase 1 gate; Phase 3 promotes it to the full service (R35).
- **R14.** Codec reality: with D2, decoding runs through Media3, which uses the device's platform decoders by default — supported sample formats depend on the device and its API level (the official Android supported media formats table documents a baseline; individual devices may add formats beyond it, so the table is a floor, not a guarantee). Container extraction is Media3's own (MP4, Matroska, WebM and more per the Media3 supported-formats documentation), so MKV playback no longer depends on WebView container behavior — but no documentation is a device guarantee for either containers or codecs. AC-3, E-AC-3, DTS/DTS-HD, and TrueHD are not in the platform baseline: they ship through the Media3 FFmpeg decoder extension built with the `ac3`, `eac3`, `dca`, and `mlp`/`truehd` decoders enabled (V2 verifies). Before shipping the extension, run the license and codec compliance check: FFmpeg builds for this use are typically LGPL-2.1-or-later (dynamic linking, license notices, and a source offer are required), and some codecs can implicate patent pools in some jurisdictions; record the compliance conclusion in the architecture record as a documented engineering note, not legal advice. Anything a device still cannot decode triggers the R15 notice instead of silent video.
- **R15.** Unsupported-audio detection and codec probe (developer settings screen). Detection rests on defined platform signals, never assumptions: (a) `Player.Listener.onTracksChanged` with per-track renderer support flags — an audio track reported as unsupported-for-type triggers the notice; (b) `PlaybackException` codes for decoder-init failure, decode failure, and unsupported-container parsing, mapped to bilingual user-facing causes; (c) a scan-time probe via `MediaCodecList.findDecoderForFormat` for the app's critical MIME types, recorded per device in the probe screen. For each extension in `AUDIO_EXTS` and `VIDEO_EXTS`, the probe screen plays a real sample and records actual decoder engagement per device. Ship a bilingual "unsupported audio track" notice instead of video that plays with no sound.
- **R16.** Snapshots: `aaz_save_snapshot` currently opens an rfd save dialog. On Android the video lives on a native surface that JS canvas cannot capture, so grab frames natively: `PixelCopy` on the player `SurfaceView` on API 26+, and `MediaMetadataRetriever.getFrameAtTime` at the current position on API 24-25. Saving needs the minSdk-24 split: on API 29+ write PNGs through `MediaStore.Images` with `RELATIVE_PATH` `Pictures/AAZ Player` (no permission needed) and return the content URI to the frontend; on API 24-28 request `WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` (declared with `maxSdkVersion="28"`) with a bilingual rationale, and if denied, save into the app-specific external Pictures directory (no permission) followed by a media scan, telling the user where the file went.
- **R17.** Remove or gate on Android: the `setSinkId` audio output picker (not available in Android WebView; the system handles routing), the `showDirectoryPicker` browser fallback, and any WebView2-specific workarounds in `player-app.js`, behind `desktop-bridge.js` feature detection.
- **R18.** Video fullscreen: immersive fullscreen on the native player surface (hide system bars via `WindowInsetsController`), with orientation lock verified on phone and tablet. Keep `WebChromeClient.onShowCustomView`/`onHideCustomView` in the Kotlin layer only for the D2 fallback paths where video renders inside the WebView. Show each file's codec and resolution in track info so playback failures are diagnosable.
- **R19.** Layout: add mobile breakpoints to `css/styles.css` (desktop-first today; window minimum 460 x 540). Phone portrait: stage on top, controls below, Library/Queue/Saved as a bottom tab bar. Phone landscape: immersive video-first. Tablet/foldable: two-pane reusing the desktop split pane (covers D3). Mirror every new layout in RTL Arabic and test both languages. Handle edge-to-edge insets (enforced at targetSdk 35) and CSS `env(safe-area-inset-*)` for notches and bars; set status and nav bar appearance per theme.
