## 6. Phase 3 (owner's Phase 3: background playback, controls, PiP, EQ, backup)

Background playback for audio and video with notification-bar and lock-screen controls, picture-in-picture, equalizer, backup and restore.

- **R35.** Player bridge and media session (the D2 core; note Android WebView has no `navigator.mediaSession` — Chromium disables it in WebView and MDN lists it unsupported — so the native session is also the only system-controls path). Phase 3 completes the player bridge whose skeleton landed in Phase 1 (R13): the Kotlin plugin's Media3 `ExoPlayer` moves into a `MediaSessionService`, activating the background UX and system controls on top of the proven skeleton. Bridge commands mirror the desktop command style: `aaz_player_load(MediaRef, positionMs?)`, play/pause/seekTo/setSpeed/setVolume, `getState`, queue advance. Events stream back to the frontend as `aaz:player-state` (playing/paused, position ticks driving the seek bar and subtitle timing, duration, buffering, current track) and `aaz:media-action` (notification, lock-screen, and headset actions). The Media3 `MediaSession` and its default media notification provide the notification-bar and lock-screen controls the owner requires, customized with artwork; the same session drives Bluetooth and hardware key events (R38). The frontend renders UI from these events only and never keeps an independent playback clock — this is what keeps WebView and native state unduplicated.
- **R36.** Background playback for audio AND video: while audio plays, or video plays as background audio (a user-toggleable "background play" setting, bilingual), the Media3 `MediaSessionService` runs in the foreground with service type `mediaPlayback`. These are manifest and build-target rules, not runtime grants: declare the service element with `android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback"` in the manifest (a missing type throws `MissingForegroundServiceTypeException` at `startForeground()`); declare `FOREGROUND_SERVICE` (install-time, required when targeting API 28+); and when targeting API 34+, also declare `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK` (install-time). The only runtime permission here is `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` on API 33+ (requested with the bilingual playback-controls rationale). Enter the foreground only from a user-visible playback action or a media-session callback, inside the platform's background-start exemptions (restricted since API 26, tighter when targeting 31+).
- **R37.** Wake locks and process survival: Media3's `setWakeMode` holds the wake lock on the player while playing with the screen off; audit it in the Phase 3 evidence (a missing wake lock is the classic 60-90 s background-death cause). Android freezes cached processes (about 10 s after becoming cached on Android 14+) and restricts background network access (Android 15+); the foreground service keeps the process in a service state and unfrozen. If T7 fails, treat it as a defect in the service or wake-lock configuration, fix it, and record the diagnosis in the architecture record — never work around it by weakening the test.
- **R38.** Audio focus and hardware controls: request `AudioFocus` on play; pause on transient loss (calls, other apps); duck or pause on transient-can-duck; resume on gain; stop and release on permanent loss; handle `ACTION_AUDIO_BECOMING_NOISY` (headphone unplug pauses). Volume keys adjust the music stream; Bluetooth and headset buttons route through the MediaSession callbacks. Expose externally-paused state to JS so the UI reflects it.
- **R39.** Picture-in-picture: declare `android:supportsPictureInPicture` (API 26+; gate at runtime per D4). In PiP mode the activity shows the native player surface only — hide the web layer per the platform's PiP guidance so the small window is all video. Map the desktop "mini player" action to PiP for video, and to an in-page floating overlay for audio (the mini player HUD already has an audio compact mode; reuse it in-page on Android). This answers the owner's question: the mini player becomes PiP on Android for video.
- **R40.** EQ and visualizer: Web Audio (`BiquadFilter`, `AnalyserNode`) cannot process native playback audio, so the 5-band studio EQ adapts to the Android audio-effects framework: attach `android.media.audiofx.Equalizer` to the ExoPlayer audio session ID (five system bands; map the existing band labels and presets, bilingual), with a designed fallback state for devices whose effect chain rejects the attach. The visualizer adapts to `android.media.audiofx.Visualizer` on the same session ID, sampled into the existing visualizer UI; if capture policy blocks it on a device, ship the EQ without the visualizer there and record it. Both get API-level probes in the Phase 3 evidence, and the EQ presets UI keeps touch targets per R21.
- **R41.** Backup and restore: JSON backup/restore export through `ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT`/`ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT` (same plugin as R34), keeping the v4 schema and pre-migration backup semantics. Surface it prominently because uninstall wipes `localStorage` (R26).
- **Phase 3 gate:** acceptance tests T7 and T11 pass; notification and lock-screen screenshots recorded; T3 regression green.

